<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kaminaljuyu</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 900 CE (approx.) · capital Kaminaljuyu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The great highland centre of the Valley of Guatemala, which controlled obsidian and jade routes and later had close ties with Teotihuacan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settled by about 800 BCE in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, Kaminaljuyu grew rich in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-preclassic">Late Preclassic</a> period from irrigated farming, using canals fed by Lake Miraflores, and from control of the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-chayal">El Chayal</a> obsidian source. In the Late Preclassic it became a large centre of earthen platform mounds, eventually numbering some 200.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Preclassic rulers were buried in elaborate tombs within earthen platforms, and carved monuments show an early writing system related to the southern Maya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epi-olmec-culture">Epi-Olmec</a> traditions. In the Early Classic, around 400 CE, buildings in the talud-tablero style and Teotihuacan-style pottery show strong links with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan</a>, whether through alliance, trade or settlement.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kaminaljuyu declined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-classic-period">Late Classic</a> and was largely abandoned by about 900 CE. Most of its mounds have been destroyed by the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, but it remains essential for understanding highland Maya origins.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kaminaljuyu.jpg" alt="Kaminaljuyu" width="320" height="152"><figcaption>Image: Madman2001 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Formative_Period_southern_Mesoamerica_2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaminaljuyu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Maya languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kaminaljuyú</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=50&amp;focus=kaminaljuyu" aria-label="Kaminaljuyu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2204 1633 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kaminaljuyu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2524 1889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2524 1889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=50&amp;focus=kaminaljuyu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

